    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Chose</a>
          <a href="#infrared-vs-traditional" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Infrared vs Traditional</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Picks</a>
          <a href="#electrical" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Wiring and Circuits</a>
          <a href="#placement-wood" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Placement, Base and Wood</a>
          <a href="#running-cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. What It Costs to Run</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Using a Sauna Safely</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Sizing</a>
          <a href="#who-should-skip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Who Should Skip One</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best home sauna for most households is the one your electrical panel can actually run. A plug-in infrared cabin like the Clearlight Sanctuary 2 avoids a 240V circuit entirely, while a traditional barrel like the Almost Heaven Essex gives real heat and steam but needs a 40-amp hard-wired run. Decide on the heat and the wiring first, and the brand comes second.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose These Saunas
          </h2>

          <p>
            Home saunas are expensive, heavy and hard to return, so we built this list from the documents that matter before you buy: the maker's electrical requirements, the listed dimensions and weight, the wood species, the heater type and the warranty. We did not lab-test these saunas. Every figure on this page comes from a manufacturer page, a manual, or a retailer or dealer listing, and we say which. Where two sources disagree, we give both.
          </p>

          <p>
            We picked across the three ways people actually buy a sauna. Infrared cabins heat your body directly with panels and usually run cooler, from about 120 to 150 degrees F. Traditional saunas heat the air and the rocks with an electric or wood stove, run hotter, and let you pour water for steam. Portable tents are a budget way to try infrared heat at home. Within each, we favored saunas whose electrical needs are clearly stated, because the circuit is the single biggest hidden cost in this category.
          </p>

          <p>
            We left out models whose specs we could not confirm. Several popular brands publish little beyond marketing copy, and sauna maker sites frequently block or rate-limit automated checks. A missing brand here is not a judgment on its quality, only on what we could verify.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prices change constantly in this category, with near-permanent sale pricing on some brands. Treat any price on this page as a snapshot from the listing we checked, and get a written quote that includes shipping, the heater, and any accessories before you commit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="infrared-vs-traditional" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Infrared vs Traditional: Pick the Heat First
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first decision is not the brand but the kind of heat. A traditional sauna heats the room's air, typically to about 160 to 195 degrees F, with an electric stove or a wood fire under a pile of rocks. You can ladle water onto the rocks to make steam, which Finns call loyly. It takes longer to warm up, often 30 to 45 minutes, and it usually needs a 240V circuit or a chimney.
          </p>

          <p>
            An infrared sauna uses carbon or ceramic panels that emit infrared energy, which warms your body more than the surrounding air. Cabin air temperatures are lower, often 120 to 150 degrees F, and warm-up is shorter. Many smaller infrared cabins run on a standard 120V outlet. There is no steam, because there are no rocks to pour water on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither is objectively better. People who grew up with traditional saunas often find infrared too gentle, and people who find high heat oppressive often prefer infrared. If you can, try both at a gym or spa before you spend $5,000 or more. If you want steam, buy traditional. If you need a plug-in installation indoors, infrared is usually easier.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Full-spectrum and low-EMF claims</h3>

          <p>
            Some infrared makers sell full-spectrum heaters that add near- and mid-infrared to far-infrared, and many advertise low EMF, quoting milligauss figures at sitting distance. These are manufacturer claims. Measurement methods vary between brands, and we have not measured them. Treat EMF numbers as one input to compare models within a brand, not as proof of a health effect either way.
          </p>
        </div>

